\section*{Asymptotics of Atomic Ionization Energy}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a mathematical result relevant to this question in the selected manuscript. Coulomb molecules are claimed to bind at most $Z+CM$ electrons, and neutral atoms to have universally bounded first ionization energy and half-electron exterior radius.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For \(M\) fixed nuclei at distinct positions \(R_j\in\mathbb R^3\), charges \(z_j\ge1\), and total \(Z=\sum_jz_j\), use the antisymmetric spin-two electron Hamiltonian
\[
 H_n=\sum_{i=1}^n\left(-\tfrac12\Delta_i-\sum_j\frac{z_j}{|x_i-R_j|}\right)
 +\sum_{i<j}\frac1{|x_i-x_j|},\qquad E_n=\inf\sigma(H_n),\quad E_0=0.
\]
The claimed constant \(C<\infty\) is universal over charges and positions, and obeys
\[
 E_n<E_{n-1}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad n\le Z+CM,
 \qquad n>Z+CM\quad\Longrightarrow\quad E_n=E_{n-1}.
\]
For a single nucleus the strict-binding bound becomes \(n\le Z+C\), including real \(Z\ge1\).

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Ground states are compared across particle sectors using common localization and screening fields. Local count bounds, changes of observation law, and estimates in nuclear voids control excess particles; deterministic comparison fields give both sides of the neutral-atom energy and radius bounds.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The source gives positive universal upper and lower bounds for the first ionization energy. It does not assert convergence or an exact Z→infinity asymptotic for removal of one electron. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 263, the documented entries give the large-$m$ ionization-energy coefficient and the generalized neutral outer-radius asymptotics. Fixed-$m$ limits and the general uniform excess-charge manuscript are not selected. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

